# FlappySwift

An implementation of Flappy Bird in Swift for iOS 15 and later.

The app uses programmatic UIKit: `SceneDelegate` creates a window for the connected `UIWindowScene` and its
`GameViewController`, which creates an `SKView` in `loadView()`. SpriteKit loads
`GameScene.sks` and runs the existing game logic in `GameScene.swift`.
`UIApplicationSceneManifest` enables the scene lifecycle. `Main.storyboard` is
retained for reference and is not loaded automatically. `LaunchScreen.storyboard`
is used only for the system launch screen.

![FlappySwift](http://i.imgur.com/1NLoToU.gif)

# Fold controls and testing

On iOS 27.1 or later, `UIHingeInteraction` feeds the device hinge angle into
`FoldJumpDetector`. Open beyond 120° to arm a gesture, then close below 90° to
perform the same action as a tap. Holding the angle below 90° does not repeat;
open beyond 120° to arm again. A fold restarts a finished game just like a tap.
Tap input remains available on all supported devices. Missing hinge data or
leaving the active scene clears the pending gesture.

For a Debug build running in a simulator, an on-screen panel shows the received
angle, armed state, and fold-trigger count. It is not included in Release builds
or on physical devices.

1. Run the FlappyBird scheme on iPhone Duo.
2. With **Fold test: off**, use Device Hub's fold controls. The **Device** readout
   shows the angle actually delivered by UIKit. If an angle slider is available,
   move it above 120°, then below 90°. A preset at exactly 90° will not trigger.
3. If Device Hub only offers presets, tap **Fold test: off** to enable the app's
   test controls. Press **130°**, then **80°**, or drag the test slider across
   those thresholds. The **Test** readout and fold counter confirm each gesture.
4. Repeat **80°**: no additional trigger. Press **130°**, then **80°** again:
   exactly one additional trigger. If the bird has already died, the gesture
   restarts the game; the next gesture makes it jump.
5. Turn fold testing off to return to real device hinge events.

The in-app test slider injects angle samples into the gameplay input path. It
does not change the simulator's physical pose and does not verify UIKit's event
delivery. It intentionally ignores live hinge input while enabled.

Run **Product > Test** (Command-U) for threshold, rearming, missing-data,
interaction-registration, and game-input regression tests.

